RT and COMMIT, witness COMMITTED. A SEAL keeps level 4 held after its commit until released (r5); other records release it on return. Failures before visibility are certain; a failure after the PENDING rename, a failed or lost COMMIT, and any failure after the commit are durability-undetermined: the lock latches and refuses every further effect, nothing follows in the operation, and the next writer's floor step and start reconcile (X3b r9 item 5). Refusals name item 8's rows, GenerationFull the busy row, and a broken composition (wrong carrier, lock order, after REV, capacity, the terminal slot, the SEAL ceiling, an unbuildable record) the invariant row. Nothing here takes the fence or a lease or writes trust state; grant-generation rollover itself is carrier_rollover.rs's. Its own crash and fault hooks (AppendStep, Fault) exist only under cfg(test); law X9's barrier points beside them in the caller's x3b.append.<kind> scope (begin, level-four, built, the witness-pending and witness-committed publications, insert, the fallible commit standing in for a COMMIT that fails before or after it lands, and the two releases; law X9 r1 item 5, unit X9-1) exist only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, and without it a point compiles to nothing and a scope or fallible point to the code it wraps. Library only."
